    "innovation": "This paper reviews recent advances in Arctic cloud research, synthesizing key satellite-based discoveries about cloud responses to sea ice loss and advocating for an integrated, \"two-way street\" approach between observations and models to improve understanding and climate projections.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "lidar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "CALIPSO",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "We argue new satellite observations from the A-train, and in particular, the spaceborne radar CloudSat [22] and lidar platform Cloud-Aerosol Lidar and Infrared Pathfinder Satellite Observation (CALIPSO) [23] have completely transformed Arctic cloud-climate studies.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "lidar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "cloud phase",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Second, polar cloud phase is not well known, and the processes underlying phase transitions in current and warming climate are hard to observe and predict from first principles. Thus, CALIPSO cloud phase observations are especially important in polar regions [30] and have been vetted against in situ airborne measurements [31].",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "lidar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "cloud vertical structure",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "CloudSat and CALIPSO have three unique advantages in polar regions. First, they are active sensors that observe cloud vertical structure. Polar clouds are geometrically and optically thinner than their lower latitude cousins, enabling CloudSat and CALIPSO to pass through the atmosphere with less attenuation and return usable signals close to the surface.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "spaceborne radar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "CloudSat",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "We argue new satellite observations from the A-train, and in particular, the spaceborne radar CloudSat [22] and lidar platform Cloud-Aerosol Lidar and Infrared Pathfinder Satellite Observation (CALIPSO) [23] have completely transformed Arctic cloud-climate studies.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "spaceborne radar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "vertical hydrometeor structure",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "As a result, vertical hydrometeor structure is observed by CloudSat and CALIPSO in polar regions and with much deeper penetration through the atmosphere than at lower latitudes.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "spaceborne radar",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "radar reflectivity",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "CloudSat measures radar reflectivity, but estimating liquid water path based on radar reflectivity requires estimating two unknowns (drop size, drop number) with one measurement (radar reflectivity).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CCSM4",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SUBSEQUENT_VERSION",
            "target_entity_instance": "CESM1-CAM5",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "We illustrate the influence of clouds, particularly liquid clouds, on Arctic sea ice simulations by comparing two CESM versions that differ only in their atmospheric model components: the Community Climate System Model version 4 (CCSM4) and CESM1-CAM5.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM1-CAM5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "INHERITED_FROM",
            "target_entity_instance": "CESM",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "The first example is basic. In the most recent version of CESM (CESM1-CAM5), Kay et al. [58] found that Greenland was too cold because of insufficient liquid containing clouds (their Fig. 7). No doubt that it is difficult to use a climate model to project Greenland melting if it is too cold in its present state.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM1-CAM5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "CAM5",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "We illustrate the influence of clouds, particularly liquid clouds, on Arctic sea ice simulations by comparing two CESM versions that differ only in their atmospheric model components: the Community Climate System Model version 4 (CCSM4) and CESM1-CAM5.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CAM5",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
